    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nThis MAN TGE Used to Haul Motocross Bikes — Now It’s a Bold, Head-Turning Home on Wheels\n24 photos\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Custom Built Campervans\nToday's featured project is based on a MAN TGE, one of the more popular vans in the UK – this one has a long wheelbase and all-wheel-drive (4Motion). It was created by Custom Built Campervans, a UK-based, family-run company specializing in, as you can tell from its name, bespoke mobile homes.\nThis is a special build in the sense that this rig used to be a camper van designed to haul motocross bikes. The interior did not work for the customer, so they asked Custom Built Campervans to bring their totally different vision to life.\nAs usual, let's start by taking a look at the outside. This rig's exterior is a head-turner, not only because it sports a variety of upgrades , but also because it's finished in bright orange paint all around. It also has a decal with black and grey stripes.\nThe vehicle rides on Black Rhino Warlord wheels wrapped in Comforser CF 1100 all-terrain tires. At the rear, you'll come across a carrier with a custom-made aluminum cargo box, as well as a spare tire carrier/ladder combo.\nClimb on top of the rig, and you'll discover a rack holding a Fiamma F80S awning with an integrated light, and solar panels.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Custom Built Campervans There are a couple of flares on the van's sides. Flares, also known as pods or capsules, are designed to increase the van width to allow you to fit a bed inside in an east to west arrangement instead of the standard north to south. This rig's CJL side pods are color matched to the van's paint and come with windows with sliding screens.\nOther notable mods are aluminum bars all around the vehicle with integrated LED light pods, a water fill-up point, and a shore power connection. There are also two high beam lights attached to the roof bar.\nOn the inside, Custom Built Campervans had to strip out everything to make room for the new interior. And it does look much better than how it used to before (see images in gallery). What's more, it has a closed layout, with a wall separating the driver's cabin from the living space.\nRegarding aesthetics, the customer opted for an oak slatted ceiling with recessed LED light strips, wood-imitating vinyl plank flooring, and grey laminated cabinetry.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Custom Built Campervans They also chose some distinctive touches, such as an acrylic backsplash with a printed picture depicting a lion. Moreover, the look is complemented by some oak elements, such as the window frames or oak butcher block countertops, and grey couch upholstery with orange accents.\nThe dividing wall has a decently sized headliner compartment, as well as a large window so you can see from one area to another.\nThe first thing you'll see as you slide open the main door is one of the two kitchen units, positioned right by the divider wall. It offers a Thetford oven, a dual-burner induction stovetop, a flip-up table that can be used outside, three cupboards, and a drawer.\nStraight ahead of the entrance, the builders devised one of the most sought-after amenities in premium camper vans: a bathroom. Hidden behind a waterproof retractable door, the bathroom boasts a Thetford toilet, a custom stainless steel shower tray, a shower, an overhead vanity compartment, and a Maxxair Dome vent fan.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Custom Built Campervans The bathroom has a unique finish: the entire back wall has an acrylic print made from a picture that the customer took while on holiday.\nNext, we have the other kitchen unit on the driver's side. It has a tall double-opening Dometic fridge with a tiny freezer compartment and a deep stainless steel sink with a cutting board cover.\nA variety of storage compartments are available on both sides, including cupboards, drawers, and cabinets.\nOn the opposite side, Custom Built Campervans added an additional counter offering more surface area and a variety of storage spots. This is also where you'll discover most of the controls for the rig's utilities, including heating controls and a customizable SavvyVan touchscreen display for controlling power and water.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Custom Built Campervans A cabinet integrated into the counter accommodates the Victron power system. Its highlights are a 460 Ah battery bank and a 3,000 W inverter. You can use three charging sources: solar power , shore power, and DC-DC charging via the alternator while the engine is running.\nAs for plumbing, the van is equipped with a freshwater tank, an under-mounted greywater tank, and an outdoor shower. The last important utility system in this rig is an overhead A/C unit that can quickly cool off the interior when needed.\nThe rear part of the interior is occupied by a spacious seating area. It has a massive U-shaped couch with a table in the middle. Five people could easily fit here. Furthermore, you get storage cubbies behind the couch backrests.\nYou can turn the couch into a small sleeping arrangement – all you need to do is drop the table to the same height as the benches and rearrange the cushions.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Custom Built Campervans However, this is not the main sleeping arrangement – you'll find that one right above. It's an elevator bed that can be raised or lowered at the press of a button. Lower it all the way down, and filler cushions on the sides extend the sleeping arrangement to make use of the entire interior width.\nAll in all, Custom Built Campervans drastically transformed this MAN TGE van into an aesthetically pleasing, super practical home on wheels. It's fascinating how much stuff the company managed to pack inside it.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ncamper van Camper van life conversion off-grid Custom Built Campervans MAN TGE\nAbout the author: Mircea Mazuru\nStarting out with a motorcycle permit just because he could get one two years earlier than a driver's license, Mircea keeps his passion for bikes (motor or no motor) alive to this day. His lifelong dream is to build his own custom camper van. Full profile\nUP NEXT Black-on-Pink Range Rover Looks Naughty, Wants You To Pay for It",
